  "answer_text": "Yes, parathyroid disease **can** cause neck pain, along with a wide range of other symptoms. Here is a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 Direct Neck-Related Symptoms\n\n- A common symptom of parathyroid disease is a **lump in the neck**, as parathyroid tumors can grow anywhere along the thyroid.\n- Patients with parathyroid disease have been found to present with **non-specific neck and shoulder pain**, alongside mild hypercalcemia and markedly elevated parathyroid hormone levels.\n- Signs and symptoms of parathyroid cancer, in particular, can include weakness, fatigue, and **a lump in the neck**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddb4 Bone & Musculoskeletal Pain (Including Neck)\n\n- The most common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism include **chronic fatigue, body aches, difficulty sleeping, bone pain**, memory loss, poor concentration, depression, and headaches.\n- Osteoporosis \u2014 a common complication of parathyroid disease \u2014 can lead to broken bones and fractures. If one of these fractures happens in the **spine or neck**, it can cause significant pain, or in severe cases, even paralysis.\n- The possibility of parathyroid disease should be considered in the presence of **multiple pathological fractures, muscle atrophy, generalized bone pain**, hypercalcemia, and a neck mass.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddea How Parathyroid Disease Causes These Problems\n\n- The parathyroid glands live behind the thyroid gland in the neck, and they produce PTH (parathyroid hormone), which controls calcium levels by acting on the kidneys and releasing calcium stored in the bones.\n- When one or more parathyroid glands make too much hormone \u2014 a condition known as **primary hyperparathyroidism** \u2014 calcium is pulled out of the bones and released into the blood, where it can cause a number of problems.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Other Common Symptoms of Parathyroid Disease\n\n- Parathyroid disease also frequently leads to **osteoporosis, kidney stones, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, and kidney failure**.\n- **Kidney stones** are a common complication, formed when calcium and other minerals in the urine combine into crystals, which can get stuck in the kidney and cause pain and swelling.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc8a Treatment\n\n- There is no medicine to cure primary hyperparathyroidism \u2014 the only way to solve the problem is with an **operation** to remove the bad parathyroid glands.\n- Surgery should be considered by most patients, because the effects of primary hyperparathyroidism on the bones usually **worsen with time**.\n\n---\n\nIf you are experiencing neck pain and suspect it may be related to your parathyroid glands, it is important to speak with your doctor. A blood test checking your **calcium** and **PTH levels** is typically the first step in diagnosis.",
